tually got dumber skills. We lose our skill to build programs and to write code ourselves. And that is a real danger I see. Even as someone who has written a lot of call myself a very experienced developer, but even as such an experienced developer, I sometimes find myself lost in code bases or, yeah, really forgetting how to implement a certain feature or how to do something which I have done in the past but maybe not in the last couple of And since I''m now using so much AI, I don''t know all the things I used to know and I''m really afraid of that, and that is a reason why I''m actively trying to reduce my amount of usage, um, of AI as a developer during developing. Um, I''m not cutting it down to zero. I''m still using AI, but I find it very important to control your usage, to take a step back from time to time, still write code on your own because you really lose your skills otherwise. All right? It''s use it or lose it, right? It''s the same for all skills in life and it is definitely also the case for writing code, but I am not just worried about AI making me or us dumber when it comes to writing code. We got Sora 2 now. We got-... video generation models for a couple of months now, but Sora 2 is really good. We got a lot of other models for generating audio. Obviously, there are large language models for We got those image generation models and I- I- I don''t know, you gotta be worried about what all these models and capabilities mean for the future of humanity, if you wanna put it like this. Deep fakes are already possible to a certain extent. Yeah, there are some errors here and there, sure, but you can really generate images or videos that are hard to distinguish from reality. And again, I got my own website that allows you to generate images with AI, but images based on YouTube just put you into different pose and give you a certain attire. But of course, you can generate all kinds of things with AI, not just put yourself into a suit. You can really generate videos now with Sora 2 that look like reality or almost like reality, and not everybody out there will see or understand that a certain video is AI. There are people that think that everything they see on TikTok or Instagram is real, and it''s not. And to be very honest, uh, even you and me who got some understanding of what''s going on in the AI space, even we will definitely often be tricked into thinking that something''s real if it''s not, and that can''t be good for humanity. And, uh, I mean, our brains are rotting since, uh, years already with all the short form content, and yeah, now I''m just an old guy. Not that old, I guess, but I grew up without TikTok and Instagram, and I know, I''m just that guy that complains about the- the future and how everything was better in the past, but I''m really worried about where this will all take us, uh, for, yeah, humanity as a whole, as I said. Because our attention spans get shorter and shorter and shorter, there are more fakes out there, there will be lots of people that use those capabilities for bad things, for scams, for fraud of all kinds, so that''s something we have to be worried and yeah, I''m just not sure if that''s all shiny and great. And don''t get me wrong. As I said, there are great use cases, um, being able to produce stock footage, uh, if you''re a video creator with help of great, though, to be fair, there is the entire copyright thing going on. That stock footage or that B-roll, I mean, not stock footage, the B-roll you''re producing, um, is of course, in the end, based on a model that was trained on likely copyrighted material, so that is a whole different thing, but the use case you unlock, being able to generate B-roll that fits your video perfectly, uh, without you searching through stock libraries for hours, that''s great. I won''t lie about that. I see other val- valuable use cases too, though again, you can always also argue if it''s really that valuable that some things get easier when t